ok(P+"2023 Paper 1 Q4",r"For $x\in[\frac1{n+1},\frac1n)$, $f(x)=\sqrt n$, so $f(x)\sqrt x\to1$ as $x\to0$. The lower bound is $\int_{x^2}^x\sqrt{\frac{1-t}{t}}dt\approx2\sqrt x-2x$ and the upper bound is $2\sqrt x$, so $g(x)/\sqrt x\to2$ by squeeze. Hence $f(x)g(x)=f(x)\sqrt x\cdot\frac{g(x)}{\sqrt x}\to2$.")
ok(P+"2023 Paper 1 Q5",r"Take $\ell_2$ through $(0,0,0)$ with direction $(1,1,1)$, and the face diagonal $\ell_1$ from $(1,0,1)$ to $(1,1,0)$, direction $(0,1,-1)$; the two lines are skew. The common normal is $(1,1,1)\times(0,1,-1)=(-2,1,1)$, so $d=\frac{|(1,0,1)\cdot(-2,1,1)|}{\sqrt6}=\frac1{\sqrt6}$. Face diagonals that meet the main diagonal give $0$, and by symmetry every skew pair gives $\frac1{\sqrt6}$, so the maximum is $\frac1{\sqrt6}$.")
ok(P+"2023 Paper 1 Q6",r"$y^2<5x$ forces $x\ge1$. The ellipse then allows $x=1$, $|y|\le2$ (5 points) and $x=2$, $|y|\le3$ (7 points): $|X|=12$, giving $\binom{12}{3}=220$ triples. Area $=\frac12|\det|$ is a positive integer exactly when the determinant is a nonzero even number. Counting gives 73 such triples, so the probability is $\frac{73}{220}$. Trap: collinear triples (area 0) must be excluded.")
ok(P+"2023 Paper 1 Q7",r"Let $P=(at^2,2at)$. The normal has slope $-t$, so $m=-t>0$, and it meets the $x$-axis at $Q=(2a+at^2,0)$. With $F=(a,0)$, area $=\frac12\cdot a(1+t^2)\cdot2a|t|=a^2m(1+m^2)=120$. Then $a=2$, $m=3$ gives $4\cdot3\cdot10=120$, so $(a,m)=(2,3)$.")
fix(P+"2023 Paper 1 Q14",r"$\det=7\alpha-2\beta-3$, which vanishes iff $\beta=\frac12(7\alpha-3)$. In that case, $-\frac32E_1+\frac72E_2$ reproduces the LHS of $E_3$ with RHS $28$. So $\gamma=28$ gives infinitely many solutions and $\gamma\ne28$ gives none. If $\beta\ne\frac12(7\alpha-3)$, the solution is unique. For $\alpha=1$, $\gamma=28$, the point $(11,-2,0)$ satisfies all three equations ($11-4=7$, $11=11$, $22+6=28$). So P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4.")
